Z={
1:"Madhusūdana says Arjuna’s seven questions show careful listening. Students in traditional schools were trained to repeat and question each word their teacher used.",
2:"Commentators note niyatātmabhiḥ, “by the self-controlled”: the question is how the disciplined can remember the Lord when the body fails. Many traditions prepare for death through daily practice; the Tibetan Book of the Dead and the Christian ars moriendi are well-known examples outside India.",
3:"Commentators note paramam, “supreme”: Brahman is the highest imperishable. Madhusūdana says the definitions are brief because they were well known to the Gītā’s first hearers. For later readers, the commentaries fill them out.",
4:"Commentators note deha-bhṛtāṃ vara, “best of the embodied”: Kṛṣṇa honours Arjuna as he reveals that the Lord dwells within the body. Madhusūdana says the Lord as adhiyajña receives every offering made in the body’s fire of digestion and breath.",
5:"Commentators note asaṃśayaḥ, “without doubt”: the promise is firm. Madhusūdana says remembrance at the end is the fruit of daily remembrance. Hospice workers in India often describe families gathering to chant the Lord’s names as a loved one dies.",
6:"Commentators note sadā, “always”: the state at the end is formed by what we dwell on always. Madhusūdana says the last thought cannot be chosen at the last moment by one who has not practised. The verse turns attention from death to daily life.",
7:"Commentators note arpita-mano-buddhiḥ, “mind and intellect offered”: both feeling and thinking are given to the Lord. Madhusūdana says this is how remembrance becomes constant. Many find it practical to link remembrance to daily acts: waking, eating, beginning work.",
8:"Commentators note nānya-gāminā, “not going elsewhere”: the trained mind stays on its object. Madhusūdana says practice makes it so. Modern research on attention shows that the ability to focus improves with regular training.",
9:"Commentators note acintya-rūpa, “of unthinkable form”: the Lord’s form is beyond imagination, yet meditated on. Madhusūdana says the descriptions guide the mind toward what is beyond them. A finger pointing at the moon is not the moon, but it helps one find it.",
10:"Commentators note yoga-bala, “the strength of yoga”: the dying yogi uses practised strength. Madhusūdana says the yogi’s breath and mind obey him even at death. The Mahābhārata describes Bhīṣma dying in this way, with full control, after teaching from his bed of arrows.",
11:"Commentators note vīta-rāgāḥ, “free from passion”: only the dispassionate enter the Imperishable. Madhusūdana says study, renunciation and discipline all aim at the same goal. Many traditions honour those who give their lives to study and practice.",
12:"Commentators note mūrdhni, “in the head”: the breath is raised to the crown. Madhusūdana says this is a yogic practice for the time of death. Later yoga texts describe the crown of the head, the brahmarandhra, as the door through which the yogi departs.",
13:"Commentators note ekākṣaram, “one syllable”: Oṃ is the shortest and fullest sacred word. Madhusūdana says chanting it while remembering the Lord unites sound and meaning. Many people chant Oṃ at the start and end of each day.",
14:"Commentators note nityaśaḥ, “daily”: constant remembrance is built day by day. Madhusūdana says for such a devotee, special techniques at death are not needed. Many find great comfort in this verse: love, not technique, is the key.",
15:"Commentators note mahātmānaḥ, “great souls”: those who reach the Lord. Madhusūdana says they do not return to the world of sorrow. The verse is often read at funerals and memorial services.",
16:"Commentators note punar-āvartinaḥ, “returning again”: even the highest heavens are temporary. Madhusūdana says only the Lord’s abode is beyond return. The verse puts every reward, however great, in perspective.",
17:"Commentators note aho-rātra-vidaḥ, “knowers of day and night”: those who understand cosmic time. Madhusūdana says this knowledge produces detachment from even the highest worlds. Some find a strange comfort in cosmic time: our troubles are very small against it.",
18:"Commentators note ahar-āgame, “at the coming of day”: creation is pictured as dawn. Madhusūdana says the unmanifest is the seed state from which the world grows. Some modern readers compare the cycles to theories of an expanding and contracting universe, though the Gītā’s view is spiritual.",
19:"Commentators note bhūta-grāmaḥ, “host of beings”: the same beings return in each cycle. Madhusūdana says this shows the power of karma. Only knowledge or devotion breaks the cycle.",
20:"Commentators note sanātana, “eternal”: the higher unmanifest is beyond all cycles. Madhusūdana says it does not perish when all beings perish. This is the goal of the seeker.",
21:"Commentators note paramāṃ gatim, “supreme goal”: the unmanifest Imperishable. Madhusūdana says reaching it, none return. Gauḍīya Vaiṣṇavas speak of Goloka, Kṛṣṇa’s own realm, and Śaivas of Kailāsa; each tradition pictures the highest home in its own way.",
22:"Commentators note pārtha, “son of Pṛthā”: Kṛṣṇa uses Arjuna’s mother’s name as he speaks of devotion. Madhusūdana says undivided devotion is the means to the Supreme Person. The bhakti saints often said that no learning is needed for this, only longing.",
23:"Commentators note anāvṛtti and āvṛtti, non-return and return. Madhusūdana says the yogi here means one who practises meditation or ritual. Many readers find the two paths easier to understand as two directions of life: toward light or toward smoke.",
24:"Commentators note brahma-vidaḥ, “knowers of Brahman”: the bright path is for those who know. Madhusūdana says the stages are guarded by deities of light. The image has inspired many to see life itself as a journey toward light.",
25:"Commentators note cāndramasaṃ jyotiḥ, “the light of the moon”: the lunar heaven is a reflected light, as the moon reflects the sun. Madhusūdana says this path is for those who did good works for reward. The moon’s borrowed light suits a borrowed, temporary reward.",
26:"Commentators note śāśvate, “eternal”: the two paths always exist. Madhusūdana says every being travels one or the other. The verse sums up the cosmology of the passage.",
27:"Commentators note naite sṛtī jānan, “knowing these two paths”: knowledge removes anxiety. Madhusūdana says the yogi chooses light by living rightly. Many teachers say: do not worry about auspicious times; make every time auspicious by right living.",
28:"Commentators note ādyam, “primal”: the yogi reaches the original abode. Madhusūdana says the yogi surpasses all merit. The verse closes chapter 8 and prepares chapter 9, the royal secret of devotion.",
}
